# Week 3 — Requirements Analysis, Specification & Quality


## 1. Requirements Quality Control (CARS Criteria Review)

To ensure high quality before modeling, candidate requirements are evaluated against the CARS framework (Clear, Accurate, Realistic, Specific) and checked for testability


### 1.1 Quality Evaluation Matrix

Req ID. Requirement Statement. Quality Defect Identified. Refined & Testable Requirement 
The system shall allow volunteers to register quickly and upload documents. Vague quickly. Lacks clear success metrics. FR-01: The system shall allow volunteers to create an account, enter skills and general availability, and upload PDF or JPEG qualification documents up to 5MB in size
The system shall check if a volunteer is vetted before letting them join. Unclear timing and missing error handling. FR-03: The system shall automatically prevent unverified volunteers from registering for restricted roles requiring background checks (e.g., Garda vetting) and display a warning message indicating missing requirements.
The application should be super fast and easy to navigate for users. Subjective super fast, easy. Untestable. *NFR-01 (Usability): At least 90% of first-time volunteer users shall complete event registration in 3 clicks or fewer from the main dashboard without administrative assistance
System responses must be quick during busy hours. Ambiguous quick,"busy hours.  NFR-02 (Performance): Event availability search queries shall display updated results in under 2.0 seconds under a simultaneous load of up to 500 active users



## 2. Prioritised Requirements Specification (MoSCoW Method)

Requirements are categorized using the MoSCoW prioritization framework to define MVP (Minimum Viable Product) scope and manage development risks.


### 2.1 Must Have (Critical for MVP)
FR01 (FR): The system shall allow volunteers to register an account and manage profile skills, availability, and qualification documents.
FR03 (FR): The system shall allow event organisers to create community events, define role capacities, and set qualification pre-requisites.
FR06 (FR): The system shall enforce role-based access control, preventing unverified volunteers from booking restricted event roles.
NFR01 (NFR): Personal volunteer contact information phone number, email shall only be visible to the event organiser managing that specific event GDPR Compliance.

### 2.2 Should Have (High Priority)
FR09 (FR): The system shall automatically send email notifications to registered volunteers within 2 minutes of an event cancellation or schedule change.
FR11 (FR):The system shall allow event organisers to export volunteer attendance rosters in CSV format.
NFR04 (NFR): Search results for available events shall load in under 2 seconds during peak usage.

### 2.3 Could Have (Desirable if Resources Permit)
FR-C01 (FR): The system shall maintain an automated waitlist for full events and notify waitlisted volunteers if a slot opens up.
FR-C02 (FR): The system shall display a calendar view showing upcoming volunteer commitments on the user dashboard.

### 2.4 Won't Have (Out of Scope for Initial Release)
WONT-01: Native mobile applications iOS/Android — a web-first responsive design will be used instead.
WONT-02: In-app direct peer-to-peer messaging between volunteers.



## 3. Traceability & Acceptance Criteria
Each requirement must link back to a stakeholder goal and include verifiable acceptance criteria to support testing


### Traceability Matrix Example
Requirement ID: REQ-MUST-03
Source Stakeholder: Privacy Officer / Event Organiser
Strategic Goal: Ensure safety, regulatory compliance, and proper Garda vetting for restricted volunteer roles
Acceptance Criteria (Given-When-Then):
Given an unverified volunteer is logged into the system
When they attempt to register for a Restricted Role e.g., Youth Activity Leader
Then the system shall block the registration, display the message Verified Garda Vetting Required, and provide a link to upload verification documents

## Reflection
Completing this analysis showed that user requests are often vague. Applying ISO 29148 and the CARS criteria helped turn words like quick or easy into measurable, testable requirements. Using the MoSCoW method made it clear what must be built first for the MVP, ensuring compliance and Garda vetting rules are prioritized over extra features
